1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,017
Why does a country with no ocean

2
00:00:02,017 --> 00:00:02,773
have a navy?

3
00:00:03,123 --> 00:00:05,922
This is Bolivia, in South America. It's

4
00:00:05,922 --> 00:00:09,151
completely landlocked. No beach, no coast, no

5
00:00:09,151 --> 00:00:09,438
sea.

6
00:00:09,788 --> 00:00:12,213
But it wasn't always that way. When

7
00:00:12,213 --> 00:00:15,123
Bolivia became independent, it had a strip

8
00:00:15,123 --> 00:00:17,410
of coast on the Pacific Ocean, in

9
00:00:17,410 --> 00:00:18,727
the Atacama Desert.

10
00:00:19,077 --> 00:00:22,571
That desert was full of valuable minerals.

11
00:00:22,571 --> 00:00:25,399
In 1879, a fight over them started

12
00:00:25,399 --> 00:00:28,477
the War of the Pacific, between Chile

13
00:00:28,477 --> 00:00:31,222
on one side, and Bolivia and Peru

14
00:00:31,222 --> 00:00:32,303
on the other.

15
00:00:32,653 --> 00:00:35,961
Chile won. Bolivia lost its entire coastline,

16
00:00:35,961 --> 00:00:39,121
about four hundred kilometers of shore, and

17
00:00:39,121 --> 00:00:41,400
has been landlocked ever since.

18
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:44,190
But Bolivia kept its navy. Today it

19
00:00:44,190 --> 00:00:47,119
has thousands of sailors who patrol rivers

20
00:00:47,119 --> 00:00:49,698
and Lake Titicaca, one of the highest

21
00:00:49,698 --> 00:00:52,278
navigable lakes in the world, high up

22
00:00:52,278 --> 00:00:53,185
in the Andes.

23
00:00:53,535 --> 00:00:56,294
Every year on March twenty third, Bolivia

24
00:00:56,294 --> 00:00:58,514
celebrates the Day of the Sea, to

25
00:00:58,514 --> 00:01:00,802
remember the coast it lost and the

26
00:01:00,802 --> 00:01:03,023
hope of getting back to the ocean

27
00:01:03,023 --> 00:01:03,561
one day.

28
00:01:03,911 --> 00:01:07,094
Losing the coast hurt Bolivia's economy. Today

29
00:01:07,094 --> 00:01:09,585
it ships many of its exports through

30
00:01:09,585 --> 00:01:12,077
ports in Chile and Peru, paying fees

31
00:01:12,077 --> 00:01:14,706
and following another country's rules.

32
00:01:15,056 --> 00:01:17,901
In 2013, Bolivia took Chile to the

33
00:01:17,901 --> 00:01:21,582
International Court of Justice, asking it to

34
00:01:21,582 --> 00:01:24,595
negotiate access to the sea. In 2018

35
00:01:24,595 --> 00:01:27,356
the court ruled that Chile had no

36
00:01:27,356 --> 00:01:29,029
obligation to do so.

37
00:01:29,379 --> 00:01:32,297
Lake Titicaca itself is amazing. It sits

38
00:01:32,297 --> 00:01:35,433
about thirty eight hundred meters above sea

39
00:01:35,433 --> 00:01:37,913
level, and the Uros people live on

40
00:01:37,913 --> 00:01:40,174
floating islands made of reeds.

41
00:01:40,524 --> 00:01:43,520
Bolivia's navy even trains its sailors there,

42
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:45,917
on a lake higher than most mountains

43
00:01:45,917 --> 00:01:46,583
in Europe.

44
00:01:46,933 --> 00:01:48,971
Should Bolivia get access to the sea

45
00:01:48,971 --> 00:01:50,783
again? Tell me what you think in

46
00:01:50,783 --> 00:01:51,519
the comments.

